Answer: # overview of theia and neuralint: techniques for detecting structural bugs in deep learning programs

## 1. introduction

deep learning (dl) programs are increasingly developed using popular dl libraries such as keras and pytorch. however, developers frequently introduce structural bugs during the design of dl programs, i.e., bugs related to the activation function, layer properties, model properties, loss function, pre-processing of data, and bugs due to missing, redundant, or wrong layers. these bugs lead to performance issues, i.e., low or stuck accuracy during training, and may have severe consequences which lead to incorrect output or poor generalization after training the dl model. detecting these flaws at an early stage of the training process has potential to save computational resources and the developer's time. among the existing approaches for localizing bugs in dl programs, two notable techniques are theia and neuralint, both of which detect bugs before training. this document describes each technique in turn and summarizes their comparative effectiveness.

## 2. theia

theia is a technique that leverages the characteristics of the training dataset along with the model's parsed source code for localizing the structural bugs in dl programs and provides suggestions to fix the bug. the scope of theia is to localize structural bugs in dl programs. theia supports two types of dl architecture, i.e., fully connected neural networks (fcnns) and convolutional neural networks (cnns) designed for regression, as well as classification tasks.

### 2.1 design

to design theia, first, a general representation of the dl program, a meta-model, that is independent of any dl libraries or frameworks is constructed. a meta-model captures the characteristics of the dataset, e.g., dimension, type of training data, and properties of the dl model, e.g., the number of convolution layers and learning rate. theia utilizes the meta-model and performs context-sensitive analysis, namely call-string analysis and parameter-sensitive analysis using the verification rules to detect the structural bugs in dl programs developed using two popular dl libraries, keras and pytorch.

### 2.2 capabilities

theia detects structural bugs at the beginning of the training process and alerts the developer with informative messages that include the bug's location and fix recommendations to improve the structure of the dl model. the design of theia is grounded in the mapping between the characteristics of the dataset and the structure of the model, together with a set of verification rules to detect the occurrence of structural bugs.

### 2.3 evaluation

theia was evaluated on 40 real-world buggy dl programs obtained from stack overflow designed for regression and classification tasks. theia successfully finds 57/75 bugs. specifically, for real-world programs from stack overflow, theia identified and localized 34/45 bugs found in 22 buggy dl programs designed for multi-class classification tasks. for binary classification and regression tasks, theia detected and localized 23/30 bugs found in 18 buggy dl programs.

### 2.4 limitations

certain defects, such as those involving optimizers with different convergence rates which cannot be determined at the early stage of training, fall outside theia's scope. identifying such bugs is a limitation of the approach; theia failed to detect 12 bugs in its benchmark for this reason. future work aims to address these bugs by integrating training monitoring into theia.

## 3. neuralint

neuralint is a state-of-the-art approach for localizing bugs in dl programs. among existing approaches, only neuralint detects bugs before training and also supports cnn architecture-related bugs, which makes it the most relevant baseline for comparison with theia. however, neuralint is less effective in practice: on the same benchmark of 40 real-world buggy dl programs, neuralint detects 17/75 bugs. specifically, neuralint detected 13/45 bugs in 22 buggy dl programs designed for multi-class classification tasks, and identified 4/30 bugs in 18 buggy dl programs designed for binary classification and regression tasks.

## 4. comparative summary

the results show that theia outperforms state-of-the-art neuralint. in total, theia successfully detected 57/75 bugs in 40 real-world buggy programs, whereas neuralint detected 17/75 bugs. both techniques operate before training begins, but theia is more effective and efficient, and can be used by developers to detect structural bugs in dl programs developed using keras and pytorch.

## 5. conclusion

theia and neuralint are techniques for automatically detecting structural bugs in dl programs prior to training. theia leverages a library-independent meta-model of the dl program, together with context-sensitive and parameter-sensitive analyses driven by verification rules, to localize structural defects caused by mistakes made by developers during the design of dl programs. neuralint, the prior state of the art, likewise detects bugs before training and supports cnn architecture-related bugs, but identifies substantially fewer defects on real-world benchmarks. developers seeking to detect structural bugs in dl programs at the beginning of the training process—thereby saving computational resources and development time—may adopt theia, as it is more effective and efficient compared to neuralint.
